\section{Usage Issues}{

  In \pkg{npRmpi}, distributed bootstrap or calibration routes that replay
  RNG states can refuse a user-supplied uniform generator even when its library
  is loaded on every worker. Select a built-in uniform generator explicitly
  with \code{RNGkind(kind="Mersenne-Twister")} before invoking such a route.
  The \code{random.seed} argument alone does not change the RNG implementation.
  See \code{\link{npRmpi.init}} for the distributed replay limitations,
  custom-normal distinction, and why disabling estimator auto-dispatch does
  not necessarily disable bootstrap fanout.
  If you are using data of mixed types, then it is advisable to use the
  \code{\link{data.frame}} function to construct your input data and not
  \code{\link{cbind}}, since \code{\link{cbind}} will typically not work as
  intended on mixed data types and will coerce the data to the same
  type.

  For \code{npRmpi}, plotting calls (including bootstrap error paths)
  are supported directly under \code{npRmpi.autodispatch}. No explicit
  MPI wrapping is required when autodispatch is enabled. Manual MPI
  control remains available for advanced workflows.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{

force.run <- nzchar(Sys.getenv("NP_RMPI_RUN_MPI_EXAMPLES_IN_CHECK"))

in.check <- !force.run && (
            nzchar(Sys.getenv("_R_CHECK_PACKAGE_NAME_")) ||
            nzchar(Sys.getenv("_R_CHECK_INTERNALS2_")) ||
            nzchar(Sys.getenv("_R_CHECK_CRAN_INCOMING_")) ||
            nzchar(Sys.getenv("_R_CHECK_EXAMPLE_TIMING_THRESHOLD_"))
          )

if (!in.check) {
npRmpi.init(nslaves=1)

# EXAMPLE 1: For this example, we load Giovanni Baiocchi's Italian GDP
# panel (see Italy for details), then create a data frame in which year
# is an ordered factor, GDP is continuous, compute bandwidths using
# likelihood cross-validation, then create a grid of data on which the
# density will be evaluated for plotting purposes

data("Italy")
with(Italy, {

data <- data.frame(ordered(year), gdp)

# Compute bandwidths using likelihood cross-validation (default). Note
# that this may take a minute or two depending on the speed of your
# computer...

bw <- npudensbw(dat=data)
fit <- npudens(tdat=data, bws=bw)

# You can always do things manually, as the following example demonstrates

# Create an evaluation data matrix

year.seq <- sort(unique(year))
gdp.seq <- seq(1,36,length=50)
data.eval <- expand.grid(year=year.seq,gdp=gdp.seq)

# Generate the estimated density computed for the evaluation data

fhat <- fitted(npudens(tdat = data, edat = data.eval, bws=bw))

# Coerce the data into a matrix for plotting with persp()

f <- matrix(fhat, length(unique(year)), 50)

# Next, create a 3D perspective plot of the PDF f

persp(as.integer(levels(year.seq)), gdp.seq, f, col="lightblue",
      ticktype="detailed", ylab="GDP", xlab="Year", zlab="Density",
      theta=300, phi=50)

# Sleep for 5 seconds so that we can examine the output...

Sys.sleep(5)

# However, plot simply streamlines this process and aids in the
# visualization process (<ctrl>-C will interrupt on *NIX systems, <esc>
# will interrupt on MS Windows systems).

plot(fit)

# plot also streamlines construction of variability bounds (<ctrl>-C
# will interrupt on *NIX systems, <esc> will interrupt on MS Windows
# systems)

plot(fit, errors = "asymptotic")

# EXAMPLE 2: For this example, we simulate multivariate data, and plot the
# partial regression surfaces for a locally linear estimator and its
# derivatives.

set.seed(123)

n <- 100

x1 <- runif(n)
x2 <- runif(n)
x3 <- runif(n)
x4 <- rbinom(n, 2, .3)

y <- 1 + x1 + x2 + x3 + x4 + rnorm(n)

X <- data.frame(x1, x2, x3, ordered(x4))

bw <- npregbw(xdat=X, ydat=y, regtype="ll", bwmethod="cv.aic")
fit <- npreg(bws=bw)

plot(fit)

# Sleep for 5 seconds so that we can examine the output...

Sys.sleep(5)

# Now plot the gradients...

plot(fit, gradients=TRUE)

# Plot the partial regression surfaces with bias-corrected bootstrapped
# nonparametric confidence intervals... this may take a minute or two
# depending on the speed of your computer as the bootstrapping must be
# completed prior to results being displayed...

plot(fit,
     errors="bootstrap",
     center="bias-corrected",
     band="simultaneous")

# EXAMPLE 3: Common extraction for a level and a derivative target.
data("cps71", package = "npRmpi")
bw <- npregbw(xdat=cps71$age, ydat=cps71$logwage,
              regtype="ll", bwmethod="cv.aic")
fit <- npreg(bws=bw)
plot.out <- plot(fit, perspective=FALSE, errors="bootstrap",
                 B=25, band="pointwise", output="plot-data")
d <- as.data.frame(plot.out, panel=1, target=1)
age.eval <- d$eval_1
logwage.eval <- d$estimate
logwage.lower.ci <- d$lower
logwage.upper.ci <- d$upper

# Corrected values are descriptive targets, not offsets to add to endpoints.
p <- plot(fit, perspective=FALSE, errors="bootstrap",
          center="bias-corrected", B=25, band="pointwise", output="data")
d <- as.data.frame(p, panel=1, target=1)
logwage.bc <- d$bias_corrected
logwage.bias <- d$estimate - d$bias_corrected

# The same extraction works for derivatives. Inspect the target identity.
g <- plot(fit, perspective=FALSE, gradients=TRUE, errors="bootstrap",
          B=25, band="pointwise", output="data")
g$targets
grad <- as.data.frame(g, panel=1, target=1)
plot(g)

# EXAMPLE 4: Variations on local polynomial conditional density
# estimation with proper = TRUE.

data("Italy")

Italy2 <- within(Italy, {
  year <- as.numeric(as.character(year))
})

# Plot only: make the plotted surface proper on the plot evaluation grid.

fhat <- npcdens(gdp ~ year, data = Italy2,
                regtype = "lp", degree = 3, nmulti = 1)

plot(fhat, proper = TRUE)

# Fit an object whose fitted values are themselves proper.

ctrl_fit <- list(
  mode = "slice",
  apply = "fitted",
  slice.grid.size = 101L,
  slice.extend.factor = 0.1
)

fhat_fit <- npcdens(
  gdp ~ year,
  data = Italy2,
  regtype = "lp",
  degree = 3,
  nmulti = 1,
  proper = TRUE,
  proper.control = ctrl_fit
)

fit_proper <- fitted(fhat_fit)
fit_raw <- fhat_fit$condens.raw

# Display the repaired and raw fitted values for cases where the raw
# fitted density is negative.

head(cbind(fit_proper, fit_raw)[which(fit_raw < 0), ])

# Predict on a common explicit y-grid for several years, and render
# those predictions proper.

g.grid <- seq(min(Italy2$gdp), max(Italy2$gdp), length.out = 200)

nd_grid <- expand.grid(
  gdp = g.grid,
  year = c(1955, 1975, 1995)
)

pred_grid <- predict(fhat, newdata = nd_grid, proper = TRUE)

# Predict on paired rows with different gdp grids by year, and still
# make the predictions proper via slice mode.

g1 <- seq(quantile(Italy2$gdp, 0.10),
          quantile(Italy2$gdp, 0.60), length.out = 60)
g2 <- seq(quantile(Italy2$gdp, 0.30),
          quantile(Italy2$gdp, 0.90), length.out = 35)

nd_slice <- rbind(
  data.frame(gdp = g1, year = rep(1960, length(g1))),
  data.frame(gdp = g2, year = rep(1985, length(g2)))
)

pred_slice <- predict(
  fhat,
  newdata = nd_slice,
  proper = TRUE,
  proper.control = list(mode = "slice")
)

# One object that carries properization for fitted values and for later
# predict() calls.

ctrl_both <- list(
  mode = "slice",
  apply = "both",
  slice.grid.size = 101L,
  slice.extend.factor = 0.1
)

fhat_both <- npcdens(
  gdp ~ year,
  data = Italy2,
  regtype = "lp",
  degree = 3,
  nmulti = 1,
  proper = TRUE,
  proper.control = ctrl_both
)

fit_both <- fitted(fhat_both)
pred_both <- predict(
  fhat_both,
  newdata = nd_slice,
  proper.control = ctrl_both
)
plot(fhat_both)
})
npRmpi.quit()
} else {
  message("Skipping MPI spawn in check context (set NP_RMPI_RUN_MPI_EXAMPLES_IN_CHECK=1).")
}
} % enddontrun
}


\section{Experimental status of bias-corrected inference}{
\strong{All bias-corrected confidence intervals and bands are experimental
(pre-beta research), incomplete, and not recommended for general-purpose
inference.} This status remains in effect unless the documentation explicitly
states otherwise for a particular method and scope. It applies wherever
bias-corrected bounds are available: asymptotic and bootstrap constructions,
including legacy methods, pointwise intervals, Bonferroni intervals and
simultaneous bands, for levels and supported derivatives.

These methods form an ongoing research project. Implementation support,
numerical checks and favorable simulation results in selected settings do not
establish generally valid coverage. Coverage can fail outside the studied
settings, and available options do not constitute a general solution to
bias-corrected inference. Method-specific assumptions, restrictions and known
failures must be considered when experimenting with these bounds.

A known limitation concerns pilot-based normal-scale, bias-corrected density
intervals, including the fixed-support beta and Gaussian constructions and
the one- and two-dimensional uniform and Epanechnikov normal constructions. These use normal critical values for
\code{band="pmzsd"} (also selected when \code{band} is omitted on these
routes). The fitted pilot reference law can substantially understate
uncertainty even when all joint bias-correction covariance terms are included.
Residual smoothing bias and sparse information can also cause poor coverage
of the true density. These limitations are not confined to numerical failures
or extreme stress tests. Data-driven bandwidth selection, including
cross-validation, does not by itself establish valid bias-corrected coverage.
Results for a particular calibrated construction do not establish coverage for
other constructions. These bounds should be used for methodological
experimentation, with independent coverage assessment for the intended setting.
}

\seealso{
  \code{\link{np.kernels}}, \code{\link{np.options}},
  \code{\link{npRmpi.init}}
}

\keyword{nonparametric}
